WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.520
He may have Aliens and T2 under his belt,&nbsp;
but James Cameron has never even attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.613
a third franchise movie. Is his impossibly&nbsp;
ambitious vision for Pandora just a pipe dream?

00:00:11.613 --> 00:00:15.660
The sequel to 2009's smash hit&nbsp;
Avatar is back at long last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.460
but fans of James Cameron's record-breaking&nbsp;
franchise have much more to look forward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.780
even after The Way of Water. Avatar&nbsp;
3 is already pretty much locked in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.740
and James Cameron has many plans&nbsp;
in place for more Na'vi thrills.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.840
This is why Avatar: The Way of Water doesn't&nbsp;
matter quite as much as you might expect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.920
a massive end-of-the-year blockbuster&nbsp;
by a filmmaker of Cameron's pedigree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.780
With a third movie in the works, the immediate&nbsp;
future of the series is already pretty clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.560
regardless of how The Way of&nbsp;
Water pans out. Because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.160
the Avatar movie you should really be&nbsp;
paying attention to is the third one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.260
Virtually everything about the Avatar franchise&nbsp;
will be riding on the third installment, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.887
won't enjoy the bump in curiosity from fans who&nbsp;
have waited over a decade to return to Pandora.

00:01:00.887 --> 00:01:05.460
Early Twitter buzz around Avatar: The&nbsp;
Way of Water has been largely positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.420
and why wouldn't it be? Piranha&nbsp;
II: The Spawning notwithstanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.140
James Cameron's history as a genre&nbsp;
sequel writer-director is peerless.

00:01:13.422 --> 00:01:15.642
"Hasta la vista, baby."

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:20.040
Though even Cameron himself admits that The&nbsp;
Way of Water is a terrible business case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.860
one that'll need to become one&nbsp;
of the most successful movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.120
in history just to break even,&nbsp;
it's hard to bet against his CV.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.920
Avatar 3, however, is where things will&nbsp;
get tricky. Cameron is a great filmmaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.280
but he's also kinda sporadic. In fact, The Way of&nbsp;
Water is his 21st directing credit in 44 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.860
which includes individual TV episodes,&nbsp;
documentaries and short films. While he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.880
been a nigh-invariable success story&nbsp;
on the director's seat, Cameron is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.680
unproven helmsman when it comes to steering&nbsp;
a franchise beyond that first sequel. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.640
he produced Terminator: Dark Fate, but&nbsp;
he has never fully committed to a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.260
franchise after a sequel — and certainly&nbsp;
not on the scale that Avatar demands.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.060
In other words, Cameron's plan is to venture&nbsp;
far beyond familiar waters. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.680
the director has laid out plans for multiple&nbsp;
big-budget sequels that are all set in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.420
same universe, something that's entirely&nbsp;
uncharted territory in his body of work. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.142
we simply won't know whether he's capable&nbsp;
of doing this until Avatar 3 is released.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.440
Let's be honest: James Cameron could have retired&nbsp;
to a tropical island after pretty much any of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.940
umpteen smash hit movies. The fact that he has&nbsp;
constantly pushed on, embraced new technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:35.340
and focused on making even more impressive films&nbsp;
is testament to his drive to surpass himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.680
Since making a sci-fi sequel is his bread and&nbsp;
butter, The Way of Water is just one dazzling step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.320
in his Avatar masterplan — but this idea can't&nbsp;
truly unfold until Avatar 3 spreads its wings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.260
As a filmmaker, Cameron certainly has the chops to&nbsp;
deliver multiple epic sci-fi works, and he's had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.880
plenty of time to draft the stories he wants to&nbsp;
tell in this world. But are there enough enticing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.400
stories to tell on Pandora? Or has Cameron become&nbsp;
so enamored by his stylish world that he fails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.320
to see there's not enough substance for his&nbsp;
multi-movie story arc? Only Avatar 3 will tell.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.580
Considering Cameron's tenacity, especially&nbsp;
when it comes to the Avatar movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.600
it's clear that he views them as his&nbsp;
biggest, boldest filmmaking move yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.760
He has chosen to commit himself to building&nbsp;
a mega-franchise from scratch. What greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.960
legacy can there be than building an entire,&nbsp;
beloved universe with your own two hands?

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.920
None of the major sci-fi franchises&nbsp;
on which Cameron has worked have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:33.540
peaked past their first two movies. This&nbsp;
creates mighty expectations for Avatar 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.340
which is the first "Avatar" movie that forces&nbsp;
Cameron beyond the "successful movie and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.480
sequel" structure, and into the franchise&nbsp;
territory he seems so keen to explore.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.380
Possibly the best point of comparison&nbsp;
to what Cameron seems to be trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.380
do is fellow sci-fi franchise chef George Lucas.

00:03:49.678 --> 00:03:50.638
"Hello there."

00:03:50.638 --> 00:03:55.560
If Cameron manages to expand his universe the&nbsp;
way Lucas crafted the original Star Wars trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.120
he might very well turn Avatar into a&nbsp;
multimedia powerhouse. If this happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.760
expect to see Pandora-centric movies and TV&nbsp;
shows long after Cameron is gone. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.420
if the filmmaker gets lost in the&nbsp;
style-over-substance mode of Lucas'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.580
prequel trilogy, his expensive experiment may&nbsp;
turn into a massive, embarrassing failure.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.260
It's pretty impressive, really. After all, it's&nbsp;
not every day that we see an established superstar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.660
filmmaker swinging for the fences the way Cameron&nbsp;
does with the Avatar movies, especially at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.640
relatively late stage of his career. When it&nbsp;
comes to Cameron's own reputation as a filmmaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:35.499
Avatar 3 could well be his best chance at&nbsp;
a home run — or an easy way to strike out.

00:04:35.499 --> 00:04:39.540
And then there's the money. James Cameron&nbsp;
has said that he is fully prepared to wrap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.240
Pandora up after Avatar 3 if the movies don't&nbsp;
turn in a pretty profit. This financial aspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.920
further increases the third Avatar movie's&nbsp;
importance in the grand scheme of things. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.720
a worst case scenario, Avatar 3 will not only&nbsp;
be responsible for its own box office — it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.040
also Pandora's last line of defense if&nbsp;
Way of the Water's numbers disappoint.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.160
There's a good chance that&nbsp;
will happen, too. Per Variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:08.460
The Way of Water's breakeven point is somewhere&nbsp;
in the vicinity of $2 billion. For comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:13.560
2022's other major long-gestating sequel,&nbsp;
the highly successful Top Gun: Maverick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.960
has made $1.48 billion so far. Should&nbsp;
The Way of Water peak at such numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.360
and assuming that Avatar 3 also comes with&nbsp;
a $2 billion profit margin, the movie would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:30.960
need to make over $2.5 billion to break even and&nbsp;
cover The Way of Water's slack. To achieve this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.560
Avatar 3 would have to become the third&nbsp;
highest-grossing movie in history.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.840
This hypothetical scenario might seem like a&nbsp;
big ask, but it's not unattainable — at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.100
if your name is James Cameron. The movie that&nbsp;
Avatar 3 would need to surpass for that number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.860
three spot is none other than Titanic, which&nbsp;
currently sits at $2.2 billion. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:56.100
topping the overall list is the original Avatar,&nbsp;
which has raked in a cool $2.92 billion over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:01.560
years, with the September 2022 release alone&nbsp;
boasting a $30-million premiere weekend.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.680
So, if anyone can make these absurd numbers work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.180
it's James Cameron. Still, it's likely that&nbsp;
even he'll be sweating for quite a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:15.120
seeing as Avatar was the embodiment of "slow&nbsp;
and steady," making a relatively meager $77&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.300
million during its opening weekend and building&nbsp;
from there. As such, even if they're successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:27.540
The Way of Water and Avatar 3 may take a long time&nbsp;
to make their billions. Will the studio grow tired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:32.580
of this high-stakes gambling with achingly slow&nbsp;
returns? Will Avatar 3 need to make an immediate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:38.100
colossal box office impact to reassure everyone&nbsp;
involved? This may be yet another reason to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.120
an eye on what might turn out to be the&nbsp;
most important movie of Cameron's career.

